# Which clause boundaries a queued CALL ON condition is offered when an
# INTERPRET fragment is running, pinned on the tree-walker's own bytes.
#
# The oracle runs a fragment in an activation whose condition queue is its own,
# and this crate runs it inside the creating activation. So the queue is shared
# here and the separation has to be reconstructed. The parts of the rule these
# rows pin:
#
#   * a condition queued BEFORE the fragment is offered no boundary inside it,
#     however many clauses the fragment has and whatever they are;
#   * a condition queued INSIDE the fragment is offered the fragment's own
#     boundaries, and the first of them, not the enclosing INTERPRET's;
#   * a condition still queued when the fragment ends dies with it, and the
#     enclosing INTERPRET clause's own boundary does not pick it up;
#   * a condition queued in an OUTER fragment survives an inner fragment and is
#     offered no boundary inside it either, so what separates the queues is a
#     nesting level rather than "a fragment is running".
#
# A rule stated only as the first part is satisfied by suppressing the fragment
# clauses' boundaries, which is what the `zq = raiser(); do; say 'body'; end`
# row is here to refuse: measured, that suppression prints `body` before the
# second handler where the oracle prints the second handler first.
#
# A rule stated without the nesting-level part is satisfied by a boolean "in a
# fragment" flag, and that is a narrower gap than it looks. The flag design was
# built and run against every row below: only the `interpret "say 1; say 2"` row
# tells it from a nesting level, and the rest, the nested-fragment row included,
# produce identical bytes under both.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE. Regenerate a
# row by running its program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 --
# ir_dual.rs refuses to run at all under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )

# A plain DO in the fragment, with a condition already queued when the
# INTERPRET clause begins. The DO has a header clause and an END clause -- both
# boundaries the oracle has for a DO in a program -- and neither of them may
# take this condition: the oracle prints `body` first and only then the second
# handler, whose SIGL is the INTERPRET clause's line 4.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
zr = raiser()
interpret 'do; say ''body''; end'
say 'after' zr
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> body
out> h2 4
out> after 5

# The same fragment, with the condition raised INSIDE it instead. Now the DO's
# header clause is exactly where the oracle delivers, ahead of `body`, so the
# fragment's boundaries are live rather than suppressed. SIGL is 3 for both
# handlers, because every clause of a fragment reports the enclosing INTERPRET
# clause's own line.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
interpret 'zq = raiser(); do; say ''body''; end'
say 'after' zq
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> h2 3
out> body
out> after 5

# No construct in the fragment at all, which is what says the rule is about the
# fragment rather than about DO. Two SAY clauses, and the condition queued
# before the INTERPRET waits past both of them.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
zr = raiser()
interpret 'say ''a''; say ''b'''
say 'after' zr
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> a
out> b
out> h2 4
out> after 5

# An IF in the fragment, whose condition clause ends before the THEN branch
# runs and is therefore another boundary the fragment offers ahead of `body`.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
zr = raiser()
interpret 'if 1 = 1 then say ''body'''
say 'after' zr
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> body
out> h2 4
out> after 5

# A controlled loop in the fragment, whose per-pass re-test clause is a boundary
# a plain DO does not have, opened by `run_repeating` rather than by the
# `Simple` arm.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
zr = raiser()
interpret 'do zi = 1 to 1; say ''body''; end'
say 'after' zr
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> body
out> h2 4
out> after 5

# A condition left queued when the fragment ends. The fragment's only clause
# queues the first, its own boundary delivers it, and the handler requeues --
# with no fragment clause left to take the requeue. The oracle never runs the
# second handler at all: the queue it is sitting in ends with the fragment, and
# the enclosing INTERPRET clause does not inherit it.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
interpret 'zq = raiser()'
say 'after' zq
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> after 5

# A second fragment after the one that left a condition queued, which is what
# says the leftover is discarded rather than merely unreachable. The two
# fragments run at the same nesting, so an implementation that only declines to
# deliver would hand the first fragment's leftover to the second one's clause
# and print the second handler after `second`.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
interpret 'zq = raiser()'
interpret 'say ''second'''
say 'after' zq
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> second
out> after 5

# The same, one fragment deeper. The requeue is left in the INNER fragment's
# queue, and neither the outer fragment nor the program picks it up. Measured
# green under the delivery-key mutation, under the discard mutation and under a
# built boolean-flag design alike, so what this row holds is the oracle's answer
# for a nested fragment rather than a mechanism the other rows leave uncovered.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
interpret 'interpret "zq = raiser()"'
say 'after' zq
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> after 5

# The row that says the key is a nesting level and not a "some fragment is
# running" flag, and the only one here that does. The condition is queued in the
# OUTER fragment and an inner fragment runs while it waits: the oracle prints
# both of the inner fragment's clauses and only then the second handler, so the
# inner fragment's boundaries are no more available to it than the program's
# are. A flag makes the inner fragment's boundaries match, delivering after `1`
# and ahead of `2` -- built and run, that is the byte difference.
#
# Neighbouring shapes do not all separate the designs, so do not shorten this
# program by eye. Measured under both designs, `interpret 'zq = raiser();
# interpret "say 1"; say 2'` prints `h1 3 / 1 / h2 3 / 2 / after 5` either way.
program
call on user c1 name h1
call on user c2 name h2
interpret 'zq = raiser(); interpret "say 1; say 2"; say 3'
say 'after' zq
exit 0
raiser:
raise user c1 return 5
h1:
say 'h1' sigl
raise user c2 return 1
h2:
say 'h2' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> h1 3
out> 1
out> 2
out> h2 3
out> 3
out> after 5
